WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:02.836
I’ve always been obsessed 
with how things sound. 

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:08.174
It’s the difference between a 
great song and an iconic recording.

00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:14.973
I am Mark Ronson.

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:17.183
And whenever someone sits 
down to play me a song,

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.310
I’m already thinking 
about the sound. 

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:23.523
Be impressive. 

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:26.943
Sound matters.

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:30.113
And when I first started out, 
I wondered, how did they do that?

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:34.284
When rap songs started 
having drum machines.

00:00:34.284 --> 00:00:35.952
That was like, 'oh I gotta
get a drum machine.'

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.328
Just like that.

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:39.247
You can turn any 
noise into music.

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:44.377
Just like that.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.213
We’re constantly 
searching for new sound.

00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:50.008
I made a whole album 
with one of these.

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:53.178
Where did you actually 
sample the seagulls?

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:55.889
I think it's just me.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:56.723
Pure genius Mark.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:57.599
Pure genius, I meant.

00:00:58.349 --> 00:00:59.309
These machines are used

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:02.604
to create the sounds of the people 
that are not in the mainstream.

00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:05.023
There’s only so much that 
we can do with language. 

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:07.358
The whole world 
opened up. 

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:11.070
You started to see 
bedroom producers.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.614
Can you do 
a little editing? 

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:16.075
The next person that is going to change 
music has no idea how to play the guitar. 

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.328
Is this allowed 
to happen? 

00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:19.996
I’ve been doing it 
wrong for thirty years!

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:24.250
I didn’t need words. 

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.252
I just needed sounds.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.588
This is a
Peavey Decade.

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.465
I can’t believe this is really 
happening right now. 

00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:33.009
You can do anything 
you want to do.

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:34.677
You start 
obsessing over it. 

00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:37.680
It doesn’t have to 
follow any format. 

00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:40.225
I believe in 
happy accidents. 

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.644
Only criteria is what? 

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:44.813
It better be funky.

00:01:58.493 --> 00:01:59.869
We’re just getting started.

